            <ArticleTitle>Unknown: Multiple painful nodules - what is the diagnosis? Chronic tophaceous gout.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gout is a common rheumatologic condition of recurrent polyarticular involvement. Tophi may be the first sign of the disease. We report a 55-year-old hypertensive, chronic alcoholic and diabetic male with a three-year history of multiple painful skin-colored nodules on the forearms, elbows, feet, posterior thighs, and popliteal fossa associated with discharging sinuses. Serum uric acid was 8.1 mg/dL (normal: 2-7.4 mg/dL). Fine needle aspiration cytology showed monosodium urate crystals, which are pathognomonic for gout, confirmed histopathalogically.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
